// Cyrus perf safety (2026-10-11, ChunkMirror incident): the PERFORMANCE section + the NxPerf API sheet. Appended to every
// generated build's system prompt while MB_PERF_SAFETY is on (default); fixed text, so it caches per version. The
// watchdog itself is injected by perf.js before javac, whatever the model writes.
const PERF_SECTION = `

PERFORMANCE (hard rule 10; the build service checks it and every build is load-tested on a real server):
10. Every piece of work must be BOUNDED. A Minecraft server has 50 ms per tick for everything; a plugin that loops over the whole world, copies changes into every chunk or spawns without a limit drops a real server to 1 TPS. So:
- Never iterate all loaded chunks, all entities or all blocks of a world (getLoadedChunks(), world.getEntities(), getLivingEntities()) in an event handler or a repeating task. Work near players instead: player.getNearbyEntities(r, r, r) / location.getNearbyEntities with r = NxPerf.radius(<configured radius>) (at most 48 blocks), only in loaded chunks (world.isChunkLoaded(x, z)); never load or generate chunks to do your work.
- Per tick: at most perf.max-work-per-tick block changes / entity updates / chunk operations (default 64). Put batches into a queue: Queue<T> q = NxPerf.queue("<feature>", item -> { ... }); q.offer(item) (false when full: drop it). Or check if (!NxPerf.take("<feature>")) break; inside a loop.
- Spawning: before every spawn call if (!NxPerf.spawnAllowed("<feature>", location.getChunk())) skip it (at most 5 per second, 20 living mobs per chunk by default). Copies/clones of mobs never spawn more copies (track a generation counter, at most 3).
- Explosions: if (!NxPerf.rate("explosions", 5)) skip; power at most 4; no explosion that triggers another without a generation limit.
- Repeating work only through NxPerf.timer("<feature>", delayTicks, periodTicks, () -> { ... }) (not runTaskTimer), period 10 ticks or more unless the request needs a smoother effect (then very little work per run). Nothing heavy every tick.
- Never react to a change your own code made (no feedback loops through your own events): wrap re-entrant work in if (!NxPerf.enter("<key>")) return; try { ... } finally { NxPerf.exit("<key>"); }. No recursion through events, no while(true).
- Remembered data is bounded: lists/maps that grow with play (logs, queues, remembered changes) have a configured maximum (drop the oldest), stored per chunk/player key so one lookup never scans everything.
- Every limit is a config.yml setting with a SAFE default (radius 16, per-tick 64 or less, mob copying off) and is clamped when read (Math.min(value, hard max)). If the request asks for unbounded work ("every chunk", "infinite", "all entities every tick"), build the bounded version with these limits and say so in modbuilder.json "limitations".
NxPerf API (already in the plugin as <your package>.nxperf.NxPerf: import dev.nxeon.plugins.<lowercase plugin name>.nxperf.NxPerf; never write or edit NxPerf files; the build service starts it in onEnable): boolean take(String budget) / take(String budget, int units) (per-tick budget, false = stop for this tick); int remaining(String budget); boolean spawnAllowed(String feature, Chunk chunk); boolean rate(String name, double perSecond); <T> NxPerf.Queue<T> queue(String name, java.util.function.Consumer<T> worker) with boolean offer(T), int size(); NxPerf.Timer timer(String name, long delayTicks, long periodTicks, Runnable work) with cancel(); boolean enter(String key) / void exit(String key); int radius(int wanted); int perTick(); int maxQueue(); boolean active() (false while paused); void onPause(Runnable) / onResume(Runnable) (clear your own caches). The watchdog pauses the plugin's heavy features when the server lags because of it; /<your command> perf status shows the numbers (do not add a "perf" subcommand yourself).`;
const PERF_ON = () => PERF.enabled();

function knowledge(mcVersion) {
  const parts = [];
  const sheet = sheetFor(mcVersion);
  if (sheet) parts.push(`API REFERENCE for Paper ${mcVersion} (verified against the jar you compile with; when it disagrees with your memory, it is right):\n${sheet}`);
  const rules = RULES_ON ? rulesFor(mcVersion) : [];
  if (rules.length) parts.push(`LESSONS FROM EARLIER FAILED BUILDS (follow every one):\n${rules.map((r) => `- ${r.rule}`).join("\n")}`);
  return parts.length ? `\n\n${parts.join("\n\n")}` : "";
}

